.lua
book.chapter(22, "Strings, bytes, and Unicode")
.endlua
.sh 2 "The representation is a byte sequence"
.pp
MetaML strings contain immutable bytes. Lengths and ordinary indices count
bytes, not Unicode characters. A Unicode scalar escape in source is encoded
as UTF-8, but that does not change the representation's indexing model.
This makes strings useful for binary protocols as well as text. It also
means that an operation safe for ASCII can split a multibyte encoding when
applied indiscriminately to international text.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "ASCII byte count" (String.length "abc") 3
Test.equal "UTF-8 byte count" (String.length "\u{e9}") 2
Test.equal "embedded zero byte" (String.to_codes "A\0B") [65, 0, 66]
Test.equal "byte reconstruction" (String.of_codes [65, 66]) "AB"]=],
    "Strings are binary-safe values")
.endlua
.pp
Separate three notions when designing a text interface: bytes in an encoding,
Unicode scalar values, and user-perceived characters or grapheme clusters.
A combining sequence can contain several scalars while appearing as one
character. A UTF-8-aware scalar counter therefore still does not determine
cursor movement or display width in a sophisticated editor. Choose the unit
that your algorithm actually needs and name it in the API.
.sh 2 "Slicing and searching have explicit boundaries"
.pp
String.sub takes a start offset, a length, and the source, clamping to the
valid byte interval. String.get returns Option for a single indexed byte.
Index_of returns an optional byte offset. These contracts differ: clamping
is convenient for previews, while an optional result can preserve evidence
that a requested position did not exist. A validator should not silently
replace checked indexing with clamped slicing if absence matters.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "clamped suffix" (String.sub 1 99 "abcd") "bcd"
Test.equal "missing byte" (String.get 99 "abcd") None
Test.equal "found byte offset" (String.index_of "bc" "abcd") (Some 1)
Test.equal "absent substring" (String.index_of "xy" "abcd") None
Test.equal "prefix check" (String.starts_with "ab" "abcd") true]=],
    "Byte-oriented access and search")
.endlua
.pp
A source span expressed in bytes can be efficient and unambiguous inside a
parser, but a user interface may need to translate it to line and display
column. Do that translation at the presentation boundary while retaining the
original byte position. Replacing byte offsets with loosely defined character
counts throughout a compiler can make slicing, diagnostics, and incremental
editing disagree about the same source location.
.sh 2 "Splitting preserves information"
.pp
String.split with a nonempty separator preserves empty fields. This is
important for delimited data: a missing middle field and a missing trailing
field can carry meaning. Splitting on an empty separator produces bytes.
String.join combines fields with a separator between adjacent elements.
These operations do not implement CSV quoting, escape processing, or a
general record format; use a parser when separators may occur inside fields.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[fields := String.split "," "a,,b,"
Test.equal "empty fields retained" fields ["a", "", "b", ""]
Test.equal "simple round trip" (String.join "," fields) "a,,b,"
Test.equal "nonoverlapping replacement" (String.replace "aa" "x" "aaaa") "xx"
Test.equal "empty search leaves input" (String.replace "" "x" "abc") "abc"
Test.equal "nonpositive repeat" (String.repeat 0 "abc") ""]=],
    "Delimited fields and replacement rules")
.endlua
.pp
Replace operates on nonoverlapping matches. Repeat returns an empty string
for nonpositive counts or empty input, and rejects oversized nonempty output
with an invalid-operation diagnostic. Repeatedly concatenating growing
immutable strings can copy substantial prefixes. For many fragments, collect
them and join once, or use Buffer when mutable binary assembly is the desired
interface. The representation should match the construction pattern.
.sh 2 "Use the UTF-8 helpers deliberately"
.pp
The installed String host module adds utf8_valid, utf8_length, utf8_chars,
codepoints, and of_codepoints. Validate arbitrary bytes before treating them
as encoded text. The portable byte operations remain useful and retain their
original contracts even after the host extension is installed. An embedding
must both install the relevant host module and permit its non-core primitives
before relying on those added operations.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[text := "a\u{e9}"
Test.check "valid UTF-8" (String.utf8_valid text)
Test.equal "scalar count" (String.utf8_length text) 2
Test.equal "scalar strings" (String.utf8_chars text) ["a", "\u{e9}"]
Test.equal "scalar numbers" (String.codepoints text) [97, 233]
Test.equal "scalar round trip" (String.of_codepoints [97, 233]) text
Test.check "invalid byte sequence" (not (String.utf8_valid (String.of_codes [255])))]=],
    "Validation and scalar-oriented operations")
.endlua
.pp
Upper, lower, and basic trimming are ASCII-oriented, not a complete Unicode
normalization or case-folding service. Byte reversal can corrupt UTF-8.
Even reversing scalar strings does not necessarily preserve grapheme clusters.
For identifiers in a language you design, specify whether spelling is ASCII,
arbitrary UTF-8, normalized Unicode, or something else. A parser cannot infer
that policy merely from the host string representation.
.sh 2 "Escaping depends on the destination"
.pp
A MetaML string literal, JSON string, URI component, shell argument, and roff
line each have different escaping rules. Escaping for one does not make text
safe or correct for another. Keep the destination encoder close to the output
boundary, and preserve unescaped domain values internally. The book's own
rofflets illustrate this principle by escaping listing bytes before emitting
roff, rather than expecting code examples to contain typesetter escapes.
.pp
Round-trip tests are useful but must state what is preserved. A parser and
renderer may preserve semantic values while normalizing whitespace and escape
spelling. A source editor needs stronger fidelity, including comments and
trivia. Choose tests that reflect the intended product: byte identity for a
binary codec, scalar identity for a Unicode conversion, or semantic identity
for normalized structured data.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read metalib/string/mod.ml and metalib/cpp/String.h with its D mirror.
Construct a string containing ASCII, a multibyte scalar, and an embedded zero,
then compare byte length, scalar length, and slices. Write a delimiter parser
that rejects empty fields instead of silently dropping them. Finally, explain
why URI encoding an entire URL is different from encoding one query value;
both operations involve strings, but the destination grammar assigns different
roles to punctuation.
